  <h2>The three questions every new seller must answer</h2>
  <p><strong>1. Is my product allowed?</strong> Most states allow shelf-stable, non-potentially-hazardous foods under cottage law. For freeze-dried products the dividing line is usually water activity (0.85 or below is the common threshold), plus categorical rules (meat, poultry, and seafood are broadly excluded).</p>
  <p><strong>2. Can I prove it?</strong> A growing number of states expect sellers to verify water activity. University extension programs have validated sub-$100 meters for cottage producers; our economics guide covers testing practice and record-keeping.</p>
  <p><strong>3. Does the math work?</strong> Electricity, packaging, absorbers, labels, candy cost, and, above all, your machine's cycle time set a floor under your pricing. Log a few real batches in the <a href="/tools/batch-log">Batch Log</a> before you print price tags.</p>
